--- Mrs. Ada Turner's diary, Friday, the 2nd of January, 1931 (0/4 key facts kept) ---
I read in the evening paper that more than 2 1-2 million men are out of work, and the coal and cotton trades are in dispute. I also read that the miners of South Wales have struck, and London hopes for a temporary settlement.

--- Mr. Arthur Hale's diary, Friday, the 2nd of January, 1931 (0/4 key facts kept) ---
I spoke with Mr. Walter Thomas about what the paper says: The New Year opens gloomily; more than two and a half million are out of work, and the coal and cotton trades are in a bad way.

--- Mr. Walter Thomas's diary, Friday, the 2nd of January, 1931 (0/3 key facts kept) ---
I read in The Times that the New Year opens gloomily, more than two and a half million being out of work, and the coal and cotton trades being in dispute; and I feared it will be a bad year for the poor.
   ☾ Ada: She dreamt that she was in heaven, and saw St. Peter.
   ☾ Arthur: He dreamt that he was balancing the ledger at a great rate, and making such a mess of it that Mr. Thomas came and rated him soundly.
   ☾ Mr. Thomas: he dreamt that he and Mr. Hale walked down to Greenwich together, and dined at the Ship.

