--- Mrs. Ada Turner's diary, Sunday, the 4th of January, 1931 (3/4 key facts kept) ---
To-day I pawned my late husband's silver watch for ten shillings, paid the rent, laid the pencil back in its place, redeemed the watch, and borrowed two shillings.

--- Mr. Arthur Hale's diary, Sunday, the 4th of January, 1931 (5/5 key facts kept) ---
To-day began the new year gloomily. More than two and a half million are out of work, and the coal and cotton trades are in a bad way. I made a wrong entry in the ledger, but corrected it, and Mr. Thomas examined the quarterly accounts and found them correct, except for one mistake.

--- Mr. Walter Thomas's diary, Sunday, the 4th of January, 1931 (2/2 key facts kept) ---
To-day I set Mr. Hale to balance the quarterly ledger; spoke to him about the New Year; found the ledger correct, but with one mistake; the office was not cleaned, Mrs. Turner again failed to come, and she told me she had found a silver pencil under a desk.
   ☾ Ada: She dreamt that she had a good bit of coal, and was sitting by a warm fire.
   ☾ Arthur: He dreamt that he was reading in a book, and suddenly found himself turned into a clerk at the Bank of England.
   ☾ Mr. Thomas: He dreamt that he was being promoted to be manager of the office.

