--- Mrs. Ada Turner's diary, Monday, the 5th of January, 1931 (1/2 key facts kept) ---
I went to Lamb's, the pawnbroker, and pawned the five shillings I had saved; then I bought bread and margarine and tea and bacon at Barker's. I was very low in spirits, and walked out alone.

--- Mr. Arthur Hale's diary, Monday, the 5th of January, 1931 (0/0 key facts kept) ---
felt melancholy at reading of the death of Princess Louise, the King's eldest sister, and was sorry to learn that her illness had recurred.

--- Mr. Walter Thomas's diary, Monday, the 5th of January, 1931 (0/0 key facts kept) ---
I read in The Times the death of the Princess Royal; and Princess Louise was stated to be showing increased weakness, although she had been seriously ill for some months. Mrs. Turner did not come to clean the office, and I gave her notice.
   ☾ Ada (forgotten by morning): She dreamt that she was cleaning an office in Queen Victoria Street, and the gentleman who employed her said, "Very well done, Mrs. Turner, very well done indeed!"
   ☾ Arthur: He dreamt that he was living in a pleasant country place, and walking in a green field path, beside a clear stream, with his little daughter Joan.
   ☾ Mr. Thomas (forgotten by morning): He dreamt that he was on holiday in Germany, and that he saw the Cathedral at Cologne by moonlight.

